years have passed since large-scale  organized child abuse was exposed in  rotherham but two whistleblowers say  senior police officers are still  avoiding scrutiny for their failings a  strategic drugs analyst at south  yorkshire police angie heal first  reported drugs gangs were also sexually  exploiting children 20 years ago so the  first report i did was in 2002 and that  was looking at crack cocaine nuisance  supply in south yorkshire and that's  when i came across the issue of child  sexual exploitation because i hadn't  heard of it before so i was really  shocked  [Music]  angie wrote three reports over four  years but felt no one was listening  decade later after an independent  inquiry found hundreds of young girls  were being sexually abused by gangs  angie complained about the failure of  senior officers to act teaming up with  youth worker jane senior who contacted  the independent office for police  conduct the iopc  i got a very angry telephone call saying  um  very clearly um that if i continue down  this path i need to be very aware  that there's actually legislation and if  vexatious complainants  continue to complain  there is legislation that could be  looking up to two years imprisonment who  was saying that you could go to prison  the aopc  jane had been awarded an mbe for  exposing the grooming gang scandal she'd  been invited to downing street and was  given a commander's award after the  successful prosecution of a gang but now  trying to expose senior police failings  she was in a battle with the police  watchdog i remember going home and  saying to my husband something along the  lines wow it's like the mafia  you know you try to go for them at the  top  and everybody around them just just  comes out fighting  last week angie and jane were vindicated  the police watchdog upheld their  complaint but the full report into  senior officers seen by sky news was not  published aspects of it were included in  a broader review called operation linden  but with no mention of high-ranking  officers it's not highlighted at all you  have to know what you're looking for it  doesn't mention its  senior officers it's not really  commented on until page 112.  it's virtually the last section of the  report  report into senior officers does  strongly criticize the failed strategy  at the top but it doesn't name officers  and makes clear there was a lack of  cooperation the iopc has limited powers  when it comes to interviewing witnesses  and they say they contacted several  retired police officers who worked in  intelligence and child protection but in  their report they say they would not  engage with us and we couldn't force  them to do so and this has been a  barrier to obtaining evidence  south yorkshire police say they accept  the recommendations of the regulator but  not criticisms where no individual is  identified the police watchdog told sky  news we're proud of the impact this work  had and the real changes in policing we  expect to see as a result our priority  has always been the welfare of the  survivors we recognize that some of the  outcomes will not be what was hoped for  but every single case we looked into has  helped inform our recommendations that  aim to ensure others do not have to go  through the same experiences  but these two women say the process for  complaining against senior officers  remains murky and is not fit for purpose  jason farrell sky news
 

